The '''Republic of Guajicutea (1768–1801)''' covers the period from the constitutional reform of 1768 through the end of the [[Volisanian Civil War]] in 1801. The period was defined by the emergence of two competing political ideologies — [[National Republicanismo]] (NR), the conservative ideology of the Sartan elite, and [[Patrimonio Diversidad]] (PD), the liberal movement that demanded citizenship and political rights for all peoples of Guajicutea. The Guaji-led [[Petran Catholic|Petran Catholic Church]], once a colonial institution, secretly supported the PD, bringing it into conflict with the Sartan-dominated state. The constitution was amended in 1784 to limit church influence and expropriate missions for urban development. The period also saw continued population growth, a flourishing of art and music, ongoing piracy management through the [[Capuyaquiran and Abyalan League]], and the end of the Volisanian Civil War, which reshaped regional politics.

== Political ideologies ==

The constitutional reform of 1768, which expanded the franchise to all men and doubled the senate, created the political space for organised ideological competition. Before the reform, the republic had been governed under a single prevailing ideology — [[National Republicanismo]] (NR), the worldview of the [[Padres de Guajicutea]] and their successors. NR was foundational around nationhood: the republic was a compact among the Sartan and Guaji peoples, built on social hierarchy and guarded liberalism. The state accepted the class structure as natural, practised economic liberalism while restricting political participation, and treated the landowning elite as the rightful governing class. Under NR, the senate was a consensus institution, not a contested one — elections were contests among landowners who shared the same assumptions about who belonged in the republic and who did not.

The reform of 1768 disrupted this consensus. The newly enfranchised non-landowning voters — rural Guaji, urban labourers, artisans, smallholders — did not share the NR's assumptions. Within a few years of the reform, a new political movement coalesced around the demand that citizenship and political participation should extend to all peoples of Guajicutea, including [[Kaftians]] and [[Bayani]] who had lived on the island for generations but remained excluded from the body politic. The movement called itself [[Patrimonio Diversidad]] (PD) — the heritage of diversity — and argued that the republic's diversity was not a hierarchy to be maintained but a heritage to be celebrated and built upon.

The PD was founded by [[Esteban Makurí]], a Guaji native who began organising through parish networks in the late 1760s. By the mid-1770s, the PD was competing for senate seats in several districtos, marking the first organised challenge to NR dominance since the republic's founding.

The PD drew its strongest support from the lower strata of Guajicutean society: former farmers, plantation workers, and rural labourers who had been enfranchised by the 1768 reform but found that the NR-dominated senate did not represent their interests. The PD's programme was radical for its time: universal citizenship for all peoples regardless of ethnic origin, expansion of political representation beyond the landowning class, and opposition to alliances with states that practised chattel slavery — a position directed squarely at the republic's closest ally, [[Lacashe]], whose plantation economy was built on enslaved [[Kaftia]]n labour.

The NR, by contrast, was the party of the elite and the general Sartan class. NR senators defended the landowning franchise as it had existed before 1768, resisted further democratisation, and valued the Lacashe alliance as a pragmatic diplomatic and commercial partnership regardless of Lacashe's domestic institutions. The NR treated the PD's anti-slavery position as naive idealism that threatened the republic's most important international relationship.

The competition between NR and PD transformed the senate from a consensus body into a contested arena. Elections after 1768 increasingly pitted NR-backed landowning candidates against PD-backed candidates from the newly enfranchised population, producing a political culture that the republic's founders had not anticipated and, in many cases, did not welcome.

== Church and state ==

The [[Petran Catholic|Sartan Petran Catholic Church]] had enjoyed free reign under the republic. The constitution of 1723 made no provision for secular governance; the Church operated schools, managed charitable institutions, and held substantial urban and rural property through its parish network. The Church's educational mission, which had spread literacy to the rural population and inadvertently triggered the peasant rebellions of the 1760s, was now seen by the NR-dominated state as a political obstruction — an institution that educated the lower strata in political awareness and then encouraged them to demand rights the state was not prepared to grant.

The conflict was sharpened by a demographic shift within the Church hierarchy. Over the decades since 1723, the Sartan Petran Catholic Church had become increasingly Guaji in its clergy. Sartan-born priests, who had dominated the hierarchy at the republic's founding, were gradually replaced by Guaji-born clergy — converts and their descendants who had entered the priesthood through the parish school system. By the 1780s, the Church hierarchy was predominantly Guaji, and the Guaji clergy brought a different perspective to the relationship between faith and politics. Where Sartan clergy had treated the political order as given, Guaji clergy were more sympathetic to the PD's arguments about inclusion and diversity, and many quietly supported the movement's goals.

The state's response came under Cónsul [[Carlos Mendoza]] (1783–1787), an NR-aligned landowner who had grown alarmed at the Church's political influence. In 1784, the senate approved a constitutional amendment that limited the Church's institutional power. The amendment restricted the Church's role in education to religious instruction, removing its authority over secular schooling; placed Church properties and missions under the jurisdiction of the distrito governments; and authorised the expropriation of Church mission lands for urban development. The amendment did not disestablish the Church — Sartan Petran Catholicism remained the republic's official religion — but it stripped the institution of the temporal power it had exercised since 1723.

The expropriation of missions was the most contentious element. Church missions in the rural districtos held substantial land, much of it productive agricultural land that the Church had accumulated through colonial-era grants. The amendment transferred these lands to distrito governments, which sold or leased them for urban development and agricultural reform. The Church, which saw the missions as both religious centres and the social infrastructure of its educational mission, treated the expropriation as a political attack disguised as a land policy.

The Guaji-led Church opposed the amendment through both public and private channels. Parish priests and bishops publicly denounced the amendment from the pulpit, while privately channelling support to the PD movement through parish networks. The Church's defence of its institutional interests and its support for political liberalisation became intertwined: the Church argued that the expropriation was not merely an administrative matter but an assault on the Guaji people's right to organise their own religious and educational life. The state, in turn, accused the Church of conflating institutional self-interest with the public good.

The conflict remained unresolved at the end of this period. The Church retained its religious authority and its pastoral network, but its temporal power was diminished. The expropriation reduced its landholdings and its capacity to operate independent schools. The political alliance between the Guaji clergy and the PD movement survived and strengthened over the period.

== Citizenship debate ==

At the republic's founding, citizenship was restricted to Sartans and Guaji. The NR treated this as foundational: the republic was a compact between the Sartan settlers and the Guaji natives, the two peoples who had built the colonial society and inherited its institutions. [[Kaftians]], descended from enslaved people brought during the Burawi colonial period, and [[Bayani]], migrants who had arrived during the Sartan colonial period, were residents of Guajicutea but not citizens. They paid taxes, laboured in the fields and workshops, and served in the military, but they had no political rights and no path to citizenship.

The PD challenged this framework directly. The PD's position was that all peoples of Guajicutea — Sartan, Guaji, Kaftian, and Bayani — were able to build society and should be recognised as citizens with full political rights. The movement argued that the distinction between "founding peoples" and "later arrivals" was a fiction: the Kaftians had been on the island for over two centuries, and the Bayani for over a century. They were not newcomers but generations-old communities whose labour had built the republic's economy.

The citizenship debate became one of the defining political controversies of the period. The NR defended the existing framework on the grounds that citizenship was a constitutional category, not a humanitarian one, and that extending citizenship to Kaftians and Bayani would dilute the political identity of the republic. The PD argued that a republic that excluded a large share of its population from citizenship was not a republic at all but an oligarchy dressed in republican forms.

The debate remained unresolved at the end of the period. The PD introduced citizenship bills in the senate in 1786, 1791, and 1796, each defeated by the NR majority. The margin of defeat narrowed with each bill, as PD senators gained seats in successive elections and public opinion shifted.

== Piracy and regional security ==

The [[Capuyaquiran and Abyalan League]], formed in 1764, continued its anti-piracy mission through this period. Coordinated naval patrols by the member states — Guajicutea, [[Lacashe]], [[Asikyira]], and the former Burawi Capuyaquiran possessions — reduced pirate activity in the major trade lanes between the member states. The league's joint courts, established to prosecute captured pirates, processed an increasing number of cases through the 1770s as patrols seized more vessels and crews.

Piracy was not eliminated, however. The Volisanian Civil War continued to destabilise the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}}. Volisanian naval authority, already weakened by the civil war, could not police the distant archipelagic waters, and the semi-autonomous viceroyalties lacked the resources or inclination to do so. Pirate bands continued to operate from uninhabited islands and hidden coves beyond the league's patrol range, targeting merchant vessels on routes to and from the Volisanian viceroyalties.

The republic's naval capacity grew slowly after the 1761 seizure of the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]]. The shipyard, now under the naval ministry, produced repaired and refitted vessels for the league's patrol fleet, though it did not yet produce new warships. The league's reliance on Guajicutean shipyard capacity gave the republic a central role in regional security, but also placed burdens on its industrial capacity that the manufacturing economy could not easily absorb.

== Regional politics ==

The period was marked by intensifying regional diplomatic activity. The republic's position in the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}} — an independent state in a region dominated by the semi-autonomous Volisanian viceroyalties and the remnants of the Burawi colonial empire — required constant diplomacy.

The closest relationship remained with [[Lacashe]], the autonomous Burawi parliamentary republic on the Abyalan coast. The two republics were the region's original independent states, and their alliance was the cornerstone of Guajicutean foreign policy. But the alliance became politically contentious after the PD emerged. Lacashe's plantation economy was built on chattel slavery, and the PD — which argued that all peoples deserved citizenship and political rights — opposed the alliance on moral grounds. The PD's anti-slavery position resonated with a growing share of the Guajicutean public, particularly among the Guaji and Kaftian populations who saw the alliance as complicity with a slave state. The NR defended the alliance as a pragmatic necessity, arguing that Guajicutea could not afford to alienate its closest partner over domestic policies that were Lacashe's internal affair.

The controversy intensified after 1789, when Lacashe declared full independence from [[Burawa]] rather than accept the Abolition of Slavery Act. The PD called for the republic to sever ties with Lacashe, but the NR-dominated senate refused. The alliance survived, but the political damage was lasting: the PD's anti-slavery stance became a defining feature of Guajicutean politics and a permanent source of friction with the NR.

Relations with [[Asikyira]] remained stable but distant. The two republics cooperated through the league on anti-piracy and regional security, but the geographic distance between them limited the depth of commercial and diplomatic exchange. Asikyira's mixed Burawi-indigenous character and its indenture-based economy made it a natural ideological partner for the PD, but the practical distance limited the relationship to diplomatic courtesy and league cooperation.

The [[Volisanian Civil War|Volisanian]] viceroyalties of [[Balisca]], [[Balboa]], and [[Tapuya]] continued to trade semi-autonomously with Guajicutea throughout the period. The civil war, which had severed effective metropolitan control, allowed the viceroyalties to conduct their own commercial and diplomatic relations. Trade with the viceroyalties was an important market for Guajicutean manufactured goods, but the instability of the civil war made the routes unreliable and the political status of the viceroyalties uncertain.

Relations with the [[Zong|Zongosphere]] deepened. Zong merchants, who had established a presence in Waribá after the 1765 emissary mission, expanded their commercial operations through the 1770s and 1780s. Zong textiles, porcelain, and manufactured goods flowed through Waribá in increasing volumes, and Guajicutean sugar, tobacco, and dyestuffs were exported to Zong markets. The deepening Zong relationship was politically controversial: the NR, which valued the Breislandic trade (despite its deterioration), viewed the Zong relationship as a threat to the republic's commercial balance, while the PD welcomed Zong trade as a diversification of the republic's economic relationships.

== Culture, art, and music ==

The period saw a flourishing of cultural and artistic production. The Guajicutean Enlightenment, which had begun in the 1740s as an intellectual circle in Tabakurí, matured into a broader cultural movement that produced literature, music, and visual art blending Sartan and Guaji traditions.

Literature in Sartan by Guaji-born authors continued to develop. Historical writing emerged as a distinct genre: the first comprehensive histories of Guajicutea, written in Sartan but drawing on Guaji oral traditions and the [[Kari Yabí]] chronicle, were produced in the 1780s. Political pamphleteering, driven by the NR-PD competition, created a new form of political literature that circulated widely through the districtos.

Music developed as the most distinctive cultural form of the period. The fusion of Sartan harmonic traditions with Guaji rhythmic structures produced a Guajicutean musical style that was neither Sartan nor Guaji but something new. The port cities of Waribá and Barikú developed different musical characters: Waribá's music reflected its cosmopolitan population, absorbing Zong and Volisanian influences alongside Sartan and Guaji elements, while Barikú's music remained closer to the military and naval traditions of the colonial period. The parish church, despite the political conflict with the state, remained a primary venue for musical performance, and the Guaji-led clergy contributed to the development of choral and instrumental traditions that blended religious and secular forms.

Visual art was less developed than literature and music but began to emerge from the Church's decorative traditions. Church painting and sculpture, produced by Guaji artisans for parish churches, developed a distinctive style that blended Sartan religious iconography with Guaji natural motifs — volcanic landscapes, sea creatures, and tropical flora. The style, though produced for religious purposes, began to influence secular art as Guajicutean painters adopted its techniques for non-religious subjects.

== Population and economy ==

Population grew from approximately 115,000 in 1768 to approximately 145,000 by 1801. Growth was driven by natural increase, continued migration, and the economic expansion of the manufacturing sector. The urban centres of Tabakurí, Waribá, and Barikú continued to grow, though the majority of the population remained rural.

The economic shift from plantation agriculture to light manufacturing, which had begun in the 1740s, accelerated during this period. The [[Astillero Real de Waribá]], under the naval ministry since 1761, became the centre of a growing industrial cluster: ship repair, timber milling, rope-making, sailcloth production, and iron fitting. Sugar refining and tobacco curing expanded as value-added processing replaced raw agricultural export. Textile production, using imported raw cotton from Zong, grew in Waribá and Tabakurí.

The shift was uneven. The landowning elite, who had invested in plantations, resisted the transition to manufacturing and used their political influence to protect plantation interests. The merchant-manufacturers of the port cities, who drove the industrial expansion, found their interests better represented by the PD than by the NR. The economic divide between plantation landowners and urban manufacturers reinforced the political divide between the two ideologies.

Trade with the Zongosphere deepened, while trade with Breisland continued to decline. The Volisanian viceroyalties remained important trading partners throughout the civil war, though their long-term political status was uncertain. The end of the war in 1801 left unclear whether the viceroyalties would remain under Volisanian authority or pursue independence, and what effect either outcome would have on the semi-autonomous trade relations that had developed during the conflict.

== End of the Volisanian Civil War (1801) ==

The [[Volisanian Civil War]] ended in 1801 after sixty-five years of conflict. The war had reshaped the entire region: the Volisanian colonial empire, which had once dominated the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}} and the Abyalan coast, was reduced to a patchwork of semi-autonomous viceroyalties, independent states, and contested territories. The political status of the viceroyalties of [[Balisca]], [[Balboa]], and [[Tapuya]] remained uncertain, as did the question of whether the restored Volisanian crown would attempt to reclaim the [[Companyia Reial del Capuyaquiran]]'s assets, including the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] seized by the republic in 1761. The [[Capuyaquiran and Abyalan League]]'s role in the post-war order was likewise unresolved, with member states discussing whether to expand its mandate beyond anti-piracy to broader regional cooperation.

The republic entered the post-war period with a larger population, a more diversified economy, and a more polarised political culture than it had possessed at the 1768 reform. The NR-PD divide, the church-state conflict, and the unresolved citizenship question defined the republic's politics at the close of the period.

